%S A214030 0,1,5,3,4,5,2,3,5,9,4,11,13,5,29,3,15,5,19,19,5,9,23,11,24,13,17,11,
%T A214030 14,29,7,7,29,15,14,11,37,19,41,19,41,5,13,19,29,23,47,11,20,49,47,27,
%U A214030 25,17,4,11,59,29,28,59,11,7,5,15,69,29,65,15,23,29,9,11,36,37,149
%N A214030 The multiplication continued fraction sequence for 2,3,2,3...
%C A214030 This is the first sequence which comes from continued fractions which cannot, so far, be looked at as an apparition sequence for a linearly recursive sequence.
%C A214030 The first term a(1), is always 0. We will always refer to the continued fraction [a,b,c] for short, when we really mean the continued fraction with quotients [a,b,c].
%C A214030 To calculate a(2), we consider [2,2], and take a(2) to be the least number of terms of the sequence 2,3,2,3,... which will have the property that 2[2,2,3,2,3,...,2] = [2^2+k,...,2^2+l], where k,l>=0. We find that 2[2,2,2]=[4,1,4], and both the first term 4 and the last term, 4 are >= 2^2, so 1 is the correct number of terms from 2,3,2,3,.... Thus we have shown that a(2)=1.
%C A214030 Next, to calculate a(3), we consider the sequence [3,2,3,...,3] and notice that if we put only one term from the sequence 2,3,2,3,... between the 3s at the ends, we get 3[3,2,3]=[3^2+1,3,2] and the last term 2 is not >= 3^2, so we consider 3[3,2,3,3]=[3^2+1,3,3,2] and 2 is not >= 3^2, so we look at 3[3,2,3,2,3]=[3^2+1,3,4,4], and 4, the last term, is still not >= 3^2. Next, we consider 3[3,2,3,2,3,3] = [3^2+1,3,4,3,4] and we see that the last term 4 is still not >= 3^2. Finally when we put 5 terms of the sequence 2,3,2,3,.. between, the 3s, we get 3[3,2,3,2,3,2,3] = [10,3,4,3,10], and now the first element 10 is >=3^2, and the last number 10 is also >=3^2, and this proves that a(3)=5. By using the PARI program below, we get the sequence a(n) above, shown in the data portion.
%C A214030 From _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Aug 26 2026: (Start)
%C A214030 This resolves a question raised above by Art DuPre. Define U(0)=0, U(1)=1, and for k>=1, U(k+1) = 2*U(k) + U(k-1) if k is odd, U(k+1) = 3*U(k) + U(k-1) if k is even. Put M(c)={{c,1},{1,0}}. One period is given by B=M(3)*M(2)={{7,3},{2,1}}. Cayley-Hamilton provides B^2-8*B+I=0 and the linear recurrence U(k+4) = 8*U(k+2) - U(k).
%C A214030 The successive projective states [U(k+1):U(k)] are obtained from [1:0] by alternately applying [u:v] -> [2u+v:u] and [u:v] -> [3u+v:u]. Modulo n, z_U(n) (see Formula) is their first return time to infinity [w:0], with gcd(w,n)=1. (End)
%H A214030 M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas), <a href="/A214030/b214030.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A214030 a(n) = z_U(n)-1, where z_U(n) = min{k>=1: n | U(k)} and U is defined above. - _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Aug 26 2026
%e A214030 From _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Aug 26 2026: (Start)
%e A214030 For n=3, the original calculation above gives: 3[3,2,3,2,3,2,3] = [10,3,4,3,10], hence a(3)=5. Equivalently, 3 divides none of the values U(1), ... , U(5), namely 1,2,7,16,55, but divides U(6)=126, hence z_U(3)=6, a(3)=5. Modulo 3, the projective orbit is [1:0] -> [2:1] -> [1:2] -> [1:1] -> [1:1] -> [0:1] -> [1:0], whose first return to infinity occurs at k=6.
%e A214030 For n=4, 4[4,2,3,2,4] = [17,1,2,1,17], and the first and last elements have common value 17>=4^2, hence a(4)=3. Equivalently, 4 divides none of U(1)=1, U(2)=2 and U(3)=7, but divides U(4)=16, hence z_U(4)=4, a(4)=3. Modulo 4, [1:0] -> [2:1] -> [3:2] -> [0:3] -> [3:0]. (End)
%o A214030 (PARI)
%o A214030 {b23(n)=local(t,m=1,s=[n]); if (n<2,0,while(1,
%o A214030 if(m%2,s=concat(s,2),s=concat(s,3));
%o A214030 t=contfracpnqn(concat(s,n));
%o A214030 t=contfrac(n*t[1,1]/t[2,1]);
%o A214030 if(t[1]<n^2||t[#t]<n^2,m++,break));m)};
%o A214030 /* To print the sequence to the screen,use */
%o A214030 for(i=1,100,print1(b23(i),", "));
%o A214030 (PARI)
%o A214030 {zUproj(n)=my(u=1,v=0,t,k=0); if(n==1,return(1)); while(1, k++; t=u; u=(if(k%2,2,3)*u+v)%n; v=t; if(v==0,return(k)))};
%o A214030 {a(n)=zUproj(n)-1};
%o A214030 for(i=1, 100, print1(a(i), ", ")); \\ _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Aug 29 2026
%Y A214030 Cf. A213891, A213892.
